  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nWetting rain is expected areawide tonight into Wednesday night.\nThere is marginal risk for severe thunderstorms, Wednesday \nafternoon and evening. Locally heavy rainfall is possible \nleading to minor urban flooding.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA full latitude trough remains over the eastern U.S. from\ntonight into Wednesday evening, with shortwaves embedded in the\nsouthwest flow aloft over the region. At the surface, a warm \nfront lifts north into Maine tonight and lifts to the north \nWednesday morning, followed by a cold front that crosses Maine \nWednesday afternoon and evening.\n\nWarm advection rain showers develops from SW to NE this \nevening. There is also a chance of thunder tonight, mainly \nsouth of a Houlton to Katahdin line. The latter is in response \nto forcing from a 50-55KT low level jet crossing Downeast Maine \nTuesday night. Any thunder should be elevated in nature with no \nCAPE, but Showalter indices around 1-3C supporting isolated- \nscattered elevated storms. This will also result in the heaviest\nrainfall tonight over Downeast Maine as well.\n\nThere should be a lull in the precipitation at some point\nWednesday morning, once the warm front finishes lifting \nthrough, then showers and thunderstorms should develop over far \nNW zones Wednesday afternoon, then press ESE across the region \ninto Wednesday evening. The region should be in the left front \nquadrant of a 100+ kt upper level jet, in addition to support \nfrom a 50-55KT low level jet near the coast. With 1000-2000 J/kg\nof CAPE, 35-50KT of bulk shear (strongest over Downeast Maine),\nalong with 1000-1500 J/kg of Downdraft CAPE, there is the \npotential for strong to severe thunderstorms Wednesday afternoon\nand evening. Accordingly SPC has the region under a Marginal \nRisk for Severe Thunderstorms during this time.\n\nIn addition should see 3/4\" to 1 1/3\" of rain across the Bangor\nRegion and Downeast Maine, with 1/3\" to 3/4\" elsewhere. Given\nPrecipitable Waters Wednesday afternoon/evening of 1.5 to 1.75\"\nalong with the aforementioned jet support, there is the \npotential for locally heavy rainfall, which could result in \nlocally higher amounts. With the best forcing over Downeast \nMaine/the Bangor Region, the best chance for any localized \nflooding of urban/known poor drainage areas is in that region. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nDangerous Rip Currents and potentially High Surf along the \nDowneast coast Wednesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nPersistent onshore flow and a strengthening southerly swell will\nbuild seas near shore to 6-9ft from Wednesday into Wednesday\nnight. The resulting rough surf and dangerous rip currents will\nmake going to area Atlantic/Gulf of Maine Beaches Hazardous.\nThere is some uncertainty as to exactly how strong the swell\nwill be and the timing of its arrival, so do not quite have the\nconfidence to issue a High Surf Advisory or a Rip Current\nStatement at this time.\n\nSituations like this have resulted in people being swept into \nthe ocean by larger waves crashing on the cliffs at Acadia.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Potential for a system moving in early next \nweek, bringing some wetting rainfall.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure lingers over the region this weekend, as low \npressure system slides along the east coast late weekend into \nearly next week. Exact track of the low pressure system is \nuncertain, but generally, models agree that it will move over \nthe Gulf of Maine. To the north, another low pressure over \nCanada moves towards the region as well. Wetting rainfall is \npossible, and there could be some thunder potential along the \npassing cold front.",
